TY  - JOUR
AU  - Liu, JK
AU  - Apfelbaum, RI
TI  - Treatment of trigeminal neuralgia
T2  - NEUROSURGERY CLINICS OF NORTH AMERICA
AB  - Numerous treatment modalities are available in the management of patients with trigeminal neuralgia. Initial treatment consists of medical management, usually therapy with carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, or phenytoin. When medical therapy becomes refractory or produces adverse side effects, other interventions must be considered. Surgical options include microvascular decompression via a retromastoid craniectomy and percutaneous trigeminal neurolysis using radiofrequency thermocoagulation, glycerol injection, or balloon compression. Stereotactic radiosurgery has emerged as a minimally invasive procedure aimed at controlling refractory pain. Because no treatment is 100% successful, the pain may recur in a patient's lifetime and require another form of treatment. It is paramount that the neurosurgeon be familiar with all the available treatments so as to provide the best treatment for an individual patient. The authors provide an overview of the current treatment modalities and their indications, advantages, risks, and complications.
